  "modules": [
    {
      "title": "Module 1 \u00b7 The Modulo Idea",
      "html": "<p>The symbol $a \\equiv b\\ (\\bmod\\ n)$ means <i>$a$ and $b$ leave the same remainder when divided by $n$</i>, equivalently, $n \\mid (a-b)$.</p><p>The <b>residues mod $n$</b> are $\\{0,1,2,\\ldots,n-1\\}$. Every integer is equivalent to exactly one of these.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>$46 \\bmod 6 = 4$ since $46 = 7\\cdot 6 + 4$.</li><li>$54 \\bmod 7 = 5$ since $54 = 7\\cdot 7 + 5$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Reduce $46$ modulo $6$.<br/><b>A:</b> 4</p><p><b>Q:</b> Reduce $54$ modulo $7$.<br/><b>A:</b> 5</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>Reduce $46$ modulo $6$. \u2014 4.</li><li>Reduce $54$ modulo $7$. \u2014 5.</li><li>Reduce $27$ modulo $12$. \u2014 3.</li><li>Reduce $-17$ modulo $9$. \u2014 1.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Reduce $17$ mod $5$.",
          "a": "$17 = 3\\cdot 5 + 2$, so $17 \\equiv 2 \\pmod 5$."
        },
        {
          "q": "Reduce $-4$ mod $6$.",
          "a": "Add 6: $-4 + 6 = 2$, so $-4 \\equiv 2 \\pmod 6$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Module 2 \u00b7 Addition, Subtraction, Multiplication mod $n$",
      "html": "<p>Reduce each operand mod $n$ first, then perform the operation, then reduce again.</p>$$(a+b)\\bmod n = ((a\\bmod n)+(b\\bmod n))\\bmod n$$$$(a\\cdot b)\\bmod n = ((a\\bmod n)\\cdot(b\\bmod n))\\bmod n$$<h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>$46 \\bmod 6 = 4$ since $46 = 7\\cdot 6 + 4$.</li><li>$54 \\bmod 7 = 5$ since $54 = 7\\cdot 7 + 5$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Reduce $46$ modulo $6$.<br/><b>A:</b> 4</p><p><b>Q:</b> Reduce $54$ modulo $7$.<br/><b>A:</b> 5</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Compute $(23+18)\\bmod 7$.",
          "a": "$23\\equiv 2,\\ 18\\equiv 4 \\pmod 7$; $2+4=6$. So $(23+18)\\equiv 6\\pmod 7$."
        },
        {
          "q": "Compute $(9\\times 11)\\bmod 4$.",
          "a": "$9\\equiv 1,\\ 11\\equiv 3 \\pmod 4$; $1\\times 3=3$. Answer: $3$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Module 3 \u00b7 Applications in Daily Life",
      "html": "<p><b>Clocks</b> use mod 12; <b>days of the week</b> use mod 7; <b>calendar months</b> use mod 12. Any repeating cycle can be modelled with modular arithmetic.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>$46 \\bmod 6 = 4$ since $46 = 7\\cdot 6 + 4$.</li><li>$54 \\bmod 7 = 5$ since $54 = 7\\cdot 7 + 5$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Reduce $46$ modulo $6$.<br/><b>A:</b> 4</p><p><b>Q:</b> Reduce $54$ modulo $7$.<br/><b>A:</b> 5</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "If today is Wednesday, what day will it be $100$ days from now?",
          "a": "Wednesday = day 3 (Sun=0). $3+100 = 103$; $103\\bmod 7 = 5$. Day 5 = Friday."
        },
        {
          "q": "The time now is 8 o'clock. What time will it be after 29 hours?",
          "a": "$(8+29)\\bmod 12 = 37 \\bmod 12 = 1$. Answer: 1 o'clock."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Revision and exam focus",
      "html": "<p>Revise the whole topic by working through its key points in order, then test yourself with the 50-question quiz for this topic. Pay attention to definitions, differences between similar terms, and questions that ask you to apply the topic rather than only recall it.</p><h4>Worked examples</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Reduce $46$ modulo $6$.<br/><b>A:</b> 4 \u2014 $46 \\bmod 6 = 4$ since $46 = 7\\cdot 6 + 4$.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Reduce $54$ modulo $7$.<br/><b>A:</b> 5 \u2014 $54 \\bmod 7 = 5$ since $54 = 7\\cdot 7 + 5$.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Reduce $27$ modulo $12$.<br/><b>A:</b> 3 \u2014 $27 \\bmod 12 = 3$ since $27 = 2\\cdot 12 + 3$.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Reduce $46$ modulo $6$.",
          "a": "4"
        },
        {
          "q": "Reduce $54$ modulo $7$.",
          "a": "5"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Key definitions",
      "html": "<p>Learn these definitions exactly as stated \u2014 they are the terms examiners use.</p><ul><li><b>residues mod $n$</b>: are $\\{0,1,2,\\ldots,n-1\\}$</li><li><b>Clocks</b>: use mod 12; days of the week use mod 7; calendar months use mod 12</li><li><b>days of the week</b>: use mod 7; calendar months use mod 12</li></ul>",
      "examples": []
    }
  ],
